Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Sub Waits at the Fifth Fret
- Story & Context: Anika begins with a long tonic drone and a slow meend toward a target note reached near the fifth major fret position. Malik programs only snare and two isolated kick events; the sub channel remains muted. Each time Anika approaches the destination but turns away through andolan, Malik withholds the bass again. Reuben enters after the third failed arrival with one low bass-oboe tone that shadows the target from below. Tara uses clay rim taps to imply the missing sixteenth-note grid. When the destination finally settles, one short sub pulse and a single group clap enter together, then disappear immediately. The track closes by repeating the same journey with no electronic bass at all, proving that the destination itself has become the drop.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close documentary image of the rudra veena neck crossing a sandstone floor, one marked fret in focus, sampler sub channel muted on a small display, clay pot and bass-oboe keys in shallow depth. No full lyrics; sparse spoken cues limited to “not yet,” “hold the fifth,” “wait for the pitch,” and “now let it land.”

Track 2: Clay Grid Under an Empty Snare
- Story & Context: Malik removes the programmed snare from every second four-bar cycle, leaving only kick fragments and silence. Tara responds by building a ghost grid from muted clay body taps, but she avoids striking where the snare would normally fall. Anika plays shorter meend figures that begin just before the missing backbeat and resolve after it, making the absence feel stretched rather than empty. Reuben uses key clicks without stable pitch for the first half, then introduces one breath-heavy low note after the clay rhythm reaches its densest point. In the final section the electronic snare returns unchanged, yet it feels displaced because the listener has learned Tara’s alternate grid.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Two unglazed clay vessels on a woven mat with chalk subdivision marks around them, one empty snare step on a drum-machine grid, rudra veena bridge and bass oboe bell nearby. Spoken fragments limited to “leave the snare empty,” “count through clay,” “do not fill the center,” and “bring it back unchanged.”

Track 3: Rain Line Stops Before the Drone
- Story & Context: The EP closer begins during the first storm after several dry weeks. Rain is audible outside the stone lattice but never sampled into a loop. Anika sustains the lowest drone strings while Malik keeps the most skeletal beat on the record: one kick, one snare, then nearly a full bar of space. Reuben answers with low breath tones that stop before each veena attack. Tara uses only fingertip taps and one hollow clay-mouth pop every eight bars. At the expected final build, Malik removes all programmed drums instead. Anika performs the longest meend on the EP through the entire electronic silence. When the pitch settles, no sub arrives. One handclap sounds, followed by a final bass-oboe breath and the natural rain outside the pavilion.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Blue-hour rain beyond carved sandstone lattice, rudra veena player seated in a dry interior pool of warm utility light, silent sampler on a low table, clay pot and bass oboe at rest. Spoken fragments limited to “rain stops here,” “drone stays inside,” “no bass after landing,” and “leave the weather last.”

